---
source: crates/aozora-cli/tests/snapshot_cli.rs
expression: "run(&[\"completions\", \"--help\"])"
---
Print a shell completion script for `bash`, `zsh`, `fish`, `powershell`, `elvish`, or `nushell` to
stdout. Generate scripts from the installed binary's command tree. Release tarballs include them
under `completions/`

Usage: aozora completions [OPTIONS] <SHELL>

Arguments:
  <SHELL>
          Shell dialect to emit a completion script for

          [possible values: bash, elvish, fish, powershell, zsh, nushell]

Options:
      --color <WHEN>
          Choose `auto`, `always`, or `never` for diagnostic color. `auto` uses terminal detection
          and honours `NO_COLOR`, `CLICOLOR`, and `CLICOLOR_FORCE`. Defaults to `.aozora.toml`'s
          `color`, then `auto`. Applies to graphical diagnostics and `fmt --diff`. `spec kinds`
          tables stay monochrome. Accepts placement after any subcommand

          Possible values:
          - auto:   Use color when stdout is a terminal, honoring `NO_COLOR`, `CLICOLOR`, and
            `CLICOLOR_FORCE`
          - always: Always use color, even when piped
          - never:  Never use color

  -v, --verbose...
          Increase stderr log verbosity with `-v` for info, `-vv` for debug, or `-vvv` for trace.
          Preserves stdout and diagnostic output. `AOZORA_LOG` overrides this setting. One `-v`
          cancels `--quiet`. Accepts placement after any subcommand

  -q, --quiet
          Restrict stderr logging to errors. Preserves stdout. One `-v` cancels this setting.
          `AOZORA_LOG` overrides it. Accepts placement after any subcommand

      --lang <LOCALE>
          Choose the human message language with a `BCP-47` tag: `en`, `ja`, or `zh`. Applies to the
          stdin guard, watch banner, and `explain`. Resolves through `--lang > AOZORA_LANG >
          .aozora.toml lang > LANG`, then English. Unknown locales use English. Preserves JSON,
          short diagnostics, codes, exit status, schemas, and source encoding. Accepts placement
          after any subcommand

  -h, --help
          Print help (see a summary with '-h')

  -V, --version
          Print version
